#{[{:activity/expected-seconds 600.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186093156} :activity/id #uuid "2b71ea3d-d342-5e39-b2f8-caade1f5c635" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050125 :topic/id #uuid "2ecbf923-b219-5c2c-8d49-2c840d7ce5aa" :topic/math-academy-id 967} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186093151 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092287 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092134 :question/id #uuid "82ba817e-a4a8-5465-b775-6cc601132b49" :question/math-academy-id "e-8383"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "756e8f32-69aa-55bb-bae5-e906e0d2c690" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049879}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092910} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092914} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317219} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317220} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317221} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317222} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360605} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186503688} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186503719}] :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating Hyperbolic Cosine"} :step/id #uuid "3eb1abcd-41b5-5663-bcb8-e235db24de72" :step/math-academy-id 31232 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186093162}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186093152 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092288 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092135 :question/id #uuid "fe7322aa-03bf-5d6f-b432-0445510448b9" :question/math-academy-id "e-8384"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "65faf418-f3c7-519d-a01c-9fa1566782f7" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092908} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092911} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317309} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317310} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317311} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360606} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360607} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186373722} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186375013} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186375577} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186503664} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186503717} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186503752} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186503756}] :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating Hyperbolic Tangent"} :step/id #uuid "af761e6d-05bd-5755-bb69-7c203849fd2d" :step/math-academy-id 31233 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186093163}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186093156 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186093453 :tutorial/content "The **hyperbolic functions** are functions defined using the exponential functions $e^x$ and $e^{-x}.$ As we will see at the end of the lesson, hyperbolic functions are closely related to trigonometric functions. \n\nThe **hyperbolic cosine** (abbreviated and pronounced \"cosh\") is defined as\n\n$$\n\\cosh x=  \\dfrac12\\left(e^x+e^{-x}\\right).\n$$\n\nThe graph of $y=\\cosh x$ is shown below.\n\n![](images/28/286e0b946edf5c8a8eb23753aaef79812d7c1e7471ab32f43eda81795fb7f47a.png)\n\nThe function $y=\\cosh x$ is not a parabola, although it may look like one.\n\nNote the following properties of $y=\\cosh x\\mathbin{:}$\n\n- Its domain is $x\\in (-\\infty, \\infty).$\n\n- Its range is $y \\geq 1.$\n\n- It is an even function.\n\nTo evaluate $\\cosh x$ at some value, say $x=2,$ we simply substitute our value into the definition:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cosh}⁡2=\\frac{1}{2}({e}^{2}+{e}^{-2})≈3.7622\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "f755a3d4-5480-58af-98eb-79632e4e5487" :tutorial/math-academy-id 5174 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "96536404-77db-5be0-a7b5-e491277534a0" :step/math-academy-id 32506 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186093151}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186093157 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092292 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092139 :question/id #uuid "b6742668-0b09-520f-bfb7-28bfa7630808" :question/math-academy-id "e-8738"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9d505a3b-b99e-5110-871f-67bd8abbe0db" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092907} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092915} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259802} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317314} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360602} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360603} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186375563} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186503689} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186503718} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186503732} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186503737}] :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating Hyperbolic Sine"} :step/id #uuid "6f9ada3a-1270-57ef-b7e9-93e0c156fced" :step/math-academy-id 32508 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186093161}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186093161 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186093456 :tutorial/content "Just as we do with trigonometric functions, we can use the basic hyperbolic functions $\\sinh x$ and $\\cosh x$ to create more hyperbolic functions.\n\nThe **hyperbolic tangent** (pronounced \"tanch\") is defined as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{tanh}⁡x=\\frac{\\operatorname{sinh}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cosh}⁡x} & =\\frac{{e}^{x}-{e}^{-x}}{{e}^{x}+{e}^{-x}}\\,\\,\\,\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe graph of $y=\\tanh x$ is shown below.\n\n![](images/ac/ac244e57465dc7588094b3b6dae74bfc954f4705be6a2f5edbba2db3e6eeaa9d.png)\n\nNote the following properties of $y=\\tanh x\\mathbin{:}$\n\n- Its domain is $x\\in (-\\infty, \\infty).$\n\n- Its range is $y \\in (-1,1).$\n\n- It has horizontal asymptotes $y=\\pm 1.$\n\n- It is an odd function.\n\nWe can write down an equivalent definition of $\\tanh{x}$ by multiplying the numerator and denominator by $e^x\\mathbin{:}$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{tanh}⁡x & =\\frac{{e}^{x}-{e}^{-x}}{{e}^{x}+{e}^{-x}} \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{x}-{e}^{-x}}{{e}^{x}+{e}^{-x}}\\,\\cdot \\,\\frac{{e}^{x}}{{e}^{x}} \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{2x}-1}{{e}^{2x}+1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe third definition of $\\tanh{x}$ can be found by multiplying the numerator and denominator of our original definition by $e^{-x}\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{tanh}⁡x & =\\frac{{e}^{x}-{e}^{-x}}{{e}^{x}+{e}^{-x}} \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{x}-{e}^{-x}}{{e}^{x}+{e}^{-x}}\\,\\cdot \\,\\frac{{e}^{-x}}{{e}^{-x}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1-{e}^{-2x}}{1+{e}^{-2x}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can use any definition of $\\tanh x$ that we like for a given situation. However, it's often convenient to use the second or third definition when evaluating a function containing $\\tanh{x},$ because they include fewer exponential terms than the first definition." :tutorial/id #uuid "7b72785b-08ed-596c-b5ce-087f1a9a30bb" :tutorial/math-academy-id 5349 :tutorial/title "Hyperbolic Tangent"} :step/id #uuid "bbbe6b95-5545-53ed-accd-35b1c0f9d7e3" :step/math-academy-id 33478 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186093152}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186093162 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186093457 :tutorial/content "The **hyperbolic sine** (pronounced \"shine\") is defined as\n\n$$\n\\sinh x= \\dfrac12\\left(e^x-e^{-x}\\right).\n$$\n\nThe graph of $y=\\sinh x$ is shown below.\n\n![](images/9b/9bf940b5f8d9686e5e188eef00ab1b9c975803efa76dad21070e0697b69fe07b.png)\n\nNote the following properties of $y=\\sinh x\\mathbin{:}$\n\n- Its domain is $x\\in (-\\infty, \\infty).$\n\n- Its range is $y \\in (-\\infty, \\infty).$\n\n- It is an odd function." :tutorial/id #uuid "0256e102-6d61-5f40-b571-71cf0b097bc5" :tutorial/math-academy-id 5350 :tutorial/title "Hyperbolic Sine"} :step/id #uuid "499cb6ba-3bb8-5878-9f84-8eb8b9375734" :step/math-academy-id 33479 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186093157}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186093163 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186093458 :tutorial/content "There is a deep connection between trigonometric and hyperbolic functions. This means that we can use a lot of our knowledge of trigonometry to solve problems containing hyperbolic functions.\n\nTo see how they are connected, we need to go back to Euler's formula:\n\n$$\ne^{\\textrm i\\theta} = \\cos\\theta + \\textrm i\\sin\\theta\n$$\n\nBy replacing $\\theta$ with $-\\theta,$ we get\n\n$$\ne^{-\\textrm i\\theta} = \\cos\\theta - \\textrm i\\sin\\theta.\n$$\n\nAdding the two equations gives\n\n$$\ne^{\\textrm i\\theta}  + e^{-\\textrm i\\theta} = 2\\cos\\theta.\n$$\n\nMaking $\\cos\\theta$ the subject, we get\n\n$$\n\\cos\\theta = \\dfrac12\\left(e^{\\textrm i\\theta}  + e^{-\\textrm i\\theta}\\right).\n$$\n\nNotice that the right-hand side is identical to $\\cosh(\\textrm i\\theta).$ This means that we have the identity\n\n$$\n\\cos\\theta = \\cosh(\\textrm i\\theta).\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "5f503ef1-e265-5556-88fa-e09f0cd4f2b8" :tutorial/math-academy-id 5351 :tutorial/title "The Relationship Between Trigonometric and Hyperbolic Cosine"} :step/id #uuid "2760223b-967b-5d7a-bf06-8aba754add4f" :step/math-academy-id 33480 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186093164}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186093164 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186093459 :tutorial/content "We can similarly derive a link between $\\sin\\theta$ and $\\sinh\\theta.$ To do this, let's recall Euler's formula once more.\n\n$$\ne^{\\textrm i\\theta} = \\cos\\theta + \\textrm i\\sin\\theta\n$$\n\nWe also have\n\n$$\ne^{-\\textrm i\\theta} = \\cos\\theta - \\textrm i\\sin\\theta.\n$$\n\nSubtracting the two equations, we get\n\n$$\ne^{\\textrm i\\theta}  - e^{-\\textrm i\\theta} = 2\\textrm i\\sin\\theta.\n$$\n\nMaking $\\sin\\theta$ the subject of the equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & =\\frac{1}{2\\text{i}}({e}^{\\text{i}\\theta }-{e}^{-\\text{i}\\theta }) \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\text{i}}{2}({e}^{\\text{i}\\theta }-{e}^{-\\text{i}\\theta }),\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives us the identity\n\n$$\n\\sin\\theta = -\\textrm i \\sinh(\\textrm i\\theta).\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "0cbbf1b5-b7b6-5f5f-bd71-38b85f438621" :tutorial/math-academy-id 5352 :tutorial/title "The Relationship Between Trigonometric and Hyperbolic Sine"} :step/id #uuid "ddc723e1-15ec-5f40-af96-e10446b0b11f" :step/math-academy-id 33481}] :activity/title "The Hyperbolic Functions" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186092451}]}
